 ],
 "literal": "Hearing only, other than that, anything at all / seeing without, many years having passed, / flawlessly accustomed friends even though they are, / \"It is rare indeed, O noble ones, to behave in accordance with that,\" saying, / do not have doubt, O people of deep wisdom! / He is without contempt, he is sweet, he is a bound friend, / he does not desire falsehood that comes to ruin fame. / When mentioning my humble name, my name / as \"the foolish Chola\", he has a great / right of love; moreover, / at such a time, he will not stay away. / He will come right now; reserve a place for him!",
 "translation": "O wise men of deep knowledge, do not doubt, saying, 'Even for those whose friendship has been flawless for many years, it is rare to act in accordance with it when they have only heard of each other and never met.' He is sweet, free of contempt, and a deeply bound friend. He never desires falsehood that ruins one's fame. When my humble name is mentioned, he has such a great right of love that he calls me 'the foolish Chola'. Moreover, at such a critical time as this, he will not stay away. He will come right now; reserve a place for him!",
 "modern": "அறிவில் சிறந்தவர்களே! ஒருவரையொருவர் நேரில் பார்க்காமல், கேள்விப்படுவதோடு மட்டும் பல ஆண்டுகள் கழிந்திருந்தாலும், அத்தகைய நட்பின்படி நடப்பது அரிது என்று எண்ணி ஐயம் கொள்ளாதீர்கள். அவன் இகழ்ச்சி இல்லாதவன், இனியவன், என்னோடு பிணைக்கப்பட்ட நண்பன். புகழைக் கெடுக்கும் பொய்யை அவன் விரும்ப மாட்டான். எனது எளிய பெயரைச் சொல்லும்போது, 'பேதைச் சோழன்' என்று உரிமையோடு அழைக்கும் அன்பு அவனுக்கு உண்டு. அதுமட்டுமல்லாமல், நான் வடக்கிருக்கும் இத்தகைய காலத்தில் அவன் வராமல் இருக்க மாட்டான். அவன் இப்போதே வந்துவிடுவான்; அவனுக்கும் ஒரு இடத்தை ஒதுக்கி வையுங்கள்.",
 "explanation": "King Kōpperuñcōḻaṉ is speaking to the wise men and court officials gathered around him as he prepares to perform vadakkiruttal (fasting to death facing north). He is waiting for his beloved friend, the poet Picirāntaiyār, whom he has never met in person. The onlookers express doubt because the two have only known each other by reputation, but the king reassures them that true friendship does not require physical meetings. He describes Picirāntaiyār's noble character, his hatred of falsehood, and the deep affection that allows him to playfully call the king 'the foolish Chola'. He is absolutely certain that his friend will arrive to join him in death, and commands them to reserve a spot for him. This poem belongs to Pāṭāṇ tiṇai and IyaṉmoḦi tuṟai, celebrating the natural qualities of a noble friend.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}